## [GAP ANALYSIS] ECDSA test never covers P-384/P-521 or the long-form DER length path

**Severity:** LOW - test-coverage gap for the custom ASN.1 encoder/decoder that replaces BouncyCastle; the current code is correct

### The Problem

```java
keyPair = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC").genKeyPair();
...
byte[] rsConcat  = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(sign, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
byte[] asn1Des   = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().concatenatedRSToASN1DER(rsConcat, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
byte[] rsConcat2 = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(asn1Des, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
Assert.assertArrayEquals(rsConcat, rsConcat2);
```

- One key pair is reused for all three algorithms. On JDK 17 the default EC curve is P-256, so `testES384` and `testES512` sign with a P-256 key. Their DER signatures are about 70-72 bytes, and `asn1derToConcatenatedRS` only takes the zero-padding branch of `integerToBytes`.
- A real P-521 signature is about 138 bytes, which needs the long-form length (`0x81`) in `ASN1Encoder.writeLength` and `ASN1Decoder.readLength`. The test never produces one.
- The assertion only checks that the conversions agree with each other. Nothing checks that the re-encoded DER is a valid signature (`Signature.verify`), so a regression that affects encoder and decoder the same way would pass.

### Theory / Proof

The server-side `BCECDSACryptoProvider` relies on BouncyCastle's DER classes. This PR replaces them in the authz-client with about 300 lines of custom ASN.1 code, and this test is its only coverage. ECDSA signature sizes per RFC 7518 section 3.4 are 64/96/132 bytes for ES256/ES384/ES512; only the first matches the key actually used here.

### What happens today vs. what will break

The code is correct today: run with matching curves, the output is byte-identical to the JDK's DER and verifies. However, a future change to `writeLength`/`readLength` long-form handling, or to `integerToBytes` for 66-byte P-521 values, would not be caught by this test.

### Research

Keycloak's server-side ECDSA tests generate a key per curve (`secp256r1`, `secp384r1`, `secp521r1`) instead of relying on the JDK default.

### Recommendation

```java
private void test(ECDSAAlgorithm algorithm, String curve) throws Exception {
    KeyPairGenerator kpg = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC");
    kpg.initialize(new ECGenParameterSpec(curve));
    KeyPair keyPair = kpg.genKeyPair();
    AuthzClientCryptoProvider prov = new AuthzClientCryptoProvider();
    byte[] data = "Something to sign".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    Signature signature = Signature.getInstance(JavaAlgorithm.getJavaAlgorithm(algorithm.name()));
    signature.initSign(keyPair.getPrivate());
    signature.update(data);
    byte[] sign = signature.sign();
    byte[] rs = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(sign, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
    Assert.assertEquals(algorithm.getSignatureLength(), rs.length);
    byte[] der = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().concatenatedRSToASN1DER(rs, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
    Signature verifier = Signature.getInstance(JavaAlgorithm.getJavaAlgorithm(algorithm.name()));
    verifier.initVerify(keyPair.getPublic());
    verifier.update(data);
    Assert.assertTrue(verifier.verify(der));
}

@Test public void testES256() throws Exception { test(ECDSAAlgorithm.ES256, "secp256r1"); }
@Test public void testES384() throws Exception { test(ECDSAAlgorithm.ES384, "secp384r1"); }
@Test public void testES512() throws Exception { test(ECDSAAlgorithm.ES512, "secp521r1"); }
```
